## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key findings indicate that U.S. trade with Africa was already on a general downward trend since 2011, primarily due to declining U.S. imports of crude oil from Africa. Economic analyses link some indirect negative effects on African economies to the Trump administration's broad tariffs on China, but these do not provide direct evidence of a significant decline in bilateral U.S.-Africa trade value. Furthermore, specific data on total U.S.-Africa trade value for the years in question (2014-2020) was not retrieved, and the implementation of AGOA remained largely active. No non-partisan analysis was cited in the evidence to conclusively attribute a significant trade decline specifically to the Trump administration.

The information still lacking to finalize this check includes direct, official trade statistics comparing the value of U.S.-Africa exports and imports for the pre-Trump period (e.g., 2014-2016) with the Trump presidency period (2017-2020). The evidence also lacks a clear causal analysis from economic institutions that explicitly separates and quantifies the impact of specific Trump-era policies from other ongoing trends, such as the long-term decline in oil imports or the 2020 pandemic.

Given the evidence, the claim that trade relations "declined significantly" under Donald Trump is not sufficiently supported. While there was a pre-existing decline and indirect negative effects from trade policies, the recorded evidence does not provide the direct trade data or causal attribution necessary to confirm the claim. Therefore, the claim is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted because the fact-check found that U.S.-Africa trade was already on a general downward trend since 2011, primarily driven by declining U.S. imports of crude oil from Africa, as noted in a [Brookings Institution analysis](https://www.brookings.edu/articles/deepening-the-united-states-africa-trade-and-investment-relationship/). While economic analysts linked the Trump administration's broad tariffs on China to indirect negative effects on African economies—such as depressed commodity prices and slower growth—per a [CSIS analysis](https://www.csis.org/analysis/innocent-bystanders-why-us-china-trade-war-hurts-african-economies), this did not constitute direct evidence of a significant bilateral trade decline. The African Growth and Opportunity Act (AGOA) remained largely active, with only a minor, targeted suspension affecting approximately 3% of Rwanda's exports to the U.S. ([USTR](https://ustr.gov/about-us/policy-offices/press-office/press-releases/2018/july/president-donald-j-trump-upholds-agoa)). Crucially, the fact-check lacked official trade statistics comparing the pre-Trump and Trump-era periods, and no non-partisan economic institution was found to have produced an analysis specifically attributing a significant trade decline to the Trump administration ([Cambridge University Press](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/30C49B343A0B32BCA46A9285EDAEC667/S2398772317000952a.pdf/defining_and_redefining_usafrica_trade_relations_during_the_trump_presidency.pdf)).